==English==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:bor|la:cōnstrūctus}}
{{root|en|ine-pro|*strew-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|cōnstrūctus}}, from {{m|la|cōnstruō||to heap together}}, from {{m|la|com-||together}} + {{m|la|struō||to heap up, pile}}. {{doublet|en|construe}}.

===Pronunciation===
'''Noun'''
* {{enPR|kŏn'strŭkt|a=UK}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈkɒn.stɹʌkt/}}
* {{enPR|kän'strŭkt|a=US}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈkɑn.stɹʌkt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-construct-noun.ogg|a=US|pos=noun}}

'''Verb'''
* {{enPR|kən-strŭkt'|a=UK,US}}, {{IPA|en|/kənˈstɹʌkt/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-construct-verb.ogg|a=US|pos=verb}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʌkt|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Something constructed from parts.
#: {{ux|en|The artwork was a '''construct''' of wire and tubes.}}
#: {{ux|en|Loops and conditional statements are '''constructs''' in computer programming.}}
# A [[concept]] or [[model]].
#: {{ux|en|Bohr's theoretical '''construct''' of the atom was soon superseded by quantum mechanics.}}
# {{lb|en|genetics}} A segment of nucleic acid, created artificially, for transplantation into a target cell or tissue.

====Synonyms====
* (''something constructed from parts''): construction
* (''concept, model''): [[concept]], [[idea]], [[model]], [[notion]], [[representation]]

